The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<text>
<text>
...
<text>
Reason:
<text>
The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
<text>
...
<text>
Reason:
<text>

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (uacce->ops->isolate_err_threshold_read || uacce->ops->isolate_err_threshold_write)
    sysfs_create_file(...);
val = uacce->ops->isolate_err_threshold_read(uacce);
ret = uacce->ops->isolate_err_threshold_write(uacce, val);
Reason:
The sysfs file for isolation strategy is created when either callback is implemented, but the show/store functions unconditionally call the respective callback without a NULL check. If only one callback exists (e.g., only read but not write, or vice versa), accessing the missing callback causes a NULL pointer dereference and system crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!uacce->ops->isolate_err_threshold_read)
    return -ENOENT;
if (!uacce->ops->isolate_err_threshold_write)
    return -ENOENT;
Reason:
These checks are added at the beginning of the show and store functions to return an appropriate error (`-ENOENT`) when the corresponding callback is not implemented. This prevents the NULL pointer dereference while still allowing sysfs creation when either callback exists, as users can safely attempt to read or write only the supported operations.

